01. First understand the concept

Imagine you run an e-commerce company.

You have:

Customer profile images
Application logs
Database backups
Old invoices
Compliance records
Disaster-recovery backups

You don't want to store everything in the same way.

For example:

Customer images → accessed frequently
Old logs → rarely accessed
Compliance records → almost never accessed
DR backups → hopefully never accessed

S3 provides different Storage Classes for these different patterns.



02. Main S3 Storage Classes

For AWS DevOps, remember these:

S3 Standard
     ↓
S3 Intelligent-Tiering
     ↓
S3 Standard-IA
     ↓
S3 One Zone-IA
     ↓
S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval
     ↓
S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval
     ↓
S3 Glacier Deep Archive

There are also newer/specialized classes such as S3 Express One Zone for very high-performance, latency-sensitive workloads.



01. S3 Standard
What is it?

The default choice for frequently accessed data.

High access
High availability
Fast access
Higher storage cost
Real-time example

Suppose you have an online shopping website:

myshop.com
   ↓
Product images
   ↓
S3 Standard

Customers continuously request:

iphone.jpg
shirt.jpg
laptop.jpg
shoe.jpg

These objects are accessed frequently.

So:

S3 Standard ✅
Typical use cases
Website assets
Images
Videos accessed frequently
Application data
Frequently accessed backups
Interview answer

S3 Standard is designed for frequently accessed data that requires high availability and low-latency access.




02. S3 Intelligent-Tiering:
This is extremely important for interviews.

Problem

Suppose you don't know how frequently users will access your data.

Example:

application-logs/

Some logs might be accessed every day.

Others might not be accessed for months.

Instead of manually deciding when to move objects, S3 Intelligent-Tiering automatically moves objects between access tiers based on access patterns.

Real-time example

You upload:

app-log-001.json

Initially:

Frequently accessed
        ↓
Frequent Access tier

Later:

Not accessed for a while
        ↓
Automatically moved to lower-cost tier

If access happens again:

Application requests object
        ↓
S3 handles the appropriate tier
When should you use it?

When:

You don't know the access pattern.

Think:

Unknown access pattern
        ↓
Intelligent-Tiering
Interview question

Q: When would you choose Intelligent-Tiering instead of Standard?

Answer:

When access patterns are unpredictable or change over time, because S3 Intelligent-Tiering automatically moves objects between access tiers to optimize storage costs.



03. S3 Standard-IA:
IA = Infrequent Access

The data isn't accessed often, but when you need it, you want immediate access.

Think:

Stored for a long time
        ↓
Rarely accessed
        ↓
But must be immediately available
Real-time example

Your company generates monthly reports:

January-report.pdf
February-report.pdf
March-report.pdf
...

Employees rarely open old reports.

But if an auditor asks:

"Show me the March report."

You need it immediately.

So:

S3 Standard-IA
Important

Standard-IA generally has:

Lower storage cost than Standard
Retrieval charges
Minimum storage-duration considerations

So don't use IA for data that is accessed constantly.



04. S3 One Zone-IA
Similar to Standard-IA, but data is stored in a single Availability Zone rather than being redundantly stored across multiple AZs.

Therefore:

Cheaper
   ↓
But less resilient to AZ-level failure
Real-time example

Suppose you have:

Thumbnail images

You can regenerate them from the original images.

If something happens to the stored thumbnails, you can recreate them.

Therefore:

Original image
       ↓
S3 Standard

Generated thumbnail
       ↓
S3 One Zone-IA
Good use cases
Re-creatable data
Secondary copies
Data that can tolerate AZ loss
Bad use case

Don't use it as your only copy of critical financial records.



05. S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval
This is for archive data that is rarely accessed but needs immediate access when required.

Think:

Rare access
      +
Milliseconds access
Real-time example



06. S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval
Previously known as S3 Glacier.

This is for data that you don't need immediately.

You can retrieve the data using different retrieval options, with retrieval time ranging from minutes to hours depending on the option.

Real-time example

Imagine a company stores:

5-year-old application logs

Normally:

Nobody accesses them.

But occasionally:

Security investigation
        ↓
Need old logs
        ↓
Retrieve from Glacier Flexible Retrieval

This is cheaper than keeping everything in Standard.
A hospital might have medical images that:

are rarely accessed
        ↓
but may suddenly be required
        ↓
during a patient/legal/audit request

Glacier Instant Retrieval can be appropriate for archive data requiring immediate access.





07. S3 Glacier Deep Archive ⭐

This is for very long-term archival.

Think:

Almost never accessed
        ↓
Lowest storage cost
        ↓
Long retrieval time acceptable
Real-time example

A bank may need to retain certain records for many years:

2018 transactions
2019 transactions
2020 transactions
...

They aren't accessed normally.

But regulatory requirements may require keeping them.

So:

Long-term compliance archive
             ↓
S3 Glacier Deep Archive

Another example:

Company backup
      ↓
7-year retention
      ↓
Almost never accessed
      ↓
Deep Archive




11. The easiest way to remember 🧠

Think about access frequency:

              FREQUENT
                 │
                 ▼
          S3 STANDARD
                 │
                 ▼
       INTELLIGENT-TIERING
                 │
                 ▼
          STANDARD-IA
                 │
                 ▼
           ONE ZONE-IA
                 │
                 ▼
      GLACIER INSTANT
                 │
                 ▼
      GLACIER FLEXIBLE
                 │
                 ▼
       GLACIER DEEP ARCHIVE
                 │
                 ▼
              RARE

But remember: this isn't simply a strict "better/worse" ladder. The right class depends on access frequency, retrieval requirements, resilience needs, and retention period.

12. Real-world DevOps architecture

Imagine you're working as an AWS DevOps engineer.

Your application generates logs:

Application
    │
    ▼
CloudWatch / logging pipeline
    │
    ▼
S3

You could implement a lifecycle strategy:

Day 0
  │
  ▼
S3 Standard
  │
  │  after some period
  ▼
S3 Standard-IA
  │
  │  later
  ▼
S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval
  │
  │  long-term archive
  ▼
S3 Glacier Deep Archive

This is called an S3 Lifecycle configuration.

The important idea is:

Instead of manually moving objects, define rules that transition objects between storage classes automatically.
